{"summary": "I tried to break scripts/tmp_janitor.py with test trees under a fresh mktemp dir in /home/kesha (--apply, now = real time + 4 days, the real /proc scan). I also ran it in dry run against the real /tmp and /var/tmp. The core protections hold: symlinks and hardlinks to outside files survive, rmtree is fd-based and safe against symlink races (avoids_symlink_attacks=True on Python 3.12.3), holders found through cwd, cmdline, maps or environ (the v322venv Django servers included) are kept, and FIFOs and sockets are never deleted.

I could make it delete four things it must not:
(1) a tree used by a running process that opened it only by a relative path from a parent cwd;
(2) git repos and linked worktrees one or more levels down, with uncommitted work;
(3) bare repos;
(4) anything at all when --root is a symlink, including a directory that is a live process's cwd.

It also misses its purpose. Any leftover socket blocks a tree for good, so the 3.1 GB /tmp/orchestra-v1170 and 4629 Chromium dirs will never be cleaned. Once orchestra-v1170 passes 3 days it will raise a TMP_JANITOR alert on every run.

The claim that "no process" uses a tree is overstated: 201 of 292 processes (173 of them root) cannot be inspected by kesha. The walk descends into foreign mounts, and bind mounts are not detected at all.

The real dry run lists 42 items, 0.00 GB, all small old files in /var/tmp plus one empty seedon dir. Each of them is acceptable under the owner's rule. Timing: 18.8 s cold, 2.6 s warm, 23 MB RSS; the /proc scan takes 0.07 s and gives 12.7k refs. The existing 19 unit tests pass on the VPS.

Side effects of my audit on the VPS: one `git status` refreshed the index of /tmp/kesha/kin (its mtime is now 12:20). The status run in /var/tmp/orchestra-v1170-matched2-20261031/baseline may also have refreshed that index. I created and deleted /tmp/janitor_time_<pid>.txt for the timing run. /home/kesha/janrev.* is deleted and no test processes are left. Nothing else was written.", "defects": [{"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 89, "severity": "major", "problem": "The Git check looks only at <candidate>/.git. A repo or linked worktree nested deeper, and any bare repo, is deleted along with its unsaved work, even though the docstring's own reason (Git trees can hold unsaved work) applies to them just the same.", "scenario": "Reproduced with --apply in my test dir: t/ng/sub/repo (git init plus an untracked unsaved.txt), t/bare.git (git init --bare) and t/wtparent/wt (linked worktree of outside/main plus dirty.txt) were all deleted. Afterwards the main repo listed the worktree as 'prunable', with branch wtb still attached. On the real VPS the same pattern exists: /var/tmp/orchestra-v1170-matched2-20261031/baseline/.git (a repo with only untracked files, no commits) and /tmp/kesha/kin/.git. /tmp/kesha also holds a CV PDF and photos. Both trees will be deleted once they are 3 days stale.", "fix": "Detect .git, and bare-repo markers (HEAD + objects/ + refs/), at any depth during the walk, and send such trees to the owner bucket. If pytest fixture repos make that too noisy, allow-list only known pytest basetemp layouts (pytest-of-*/pytest-N, popen-gw*). Alternatively, treat a nested repo as safe only when `git status --porcelain` is empty and nothing is unpushed.", "verified": "ran code: test tree under a mktemp dir in /home/kesha with --apply; find on the real /tmp and /var/tmp"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 198, "severity": "major", "problem": "Roots are not canonicalised. The holder check compares the candidate's spelled path with what /proc shows, which is always the resolved path, so a symlinked --root (or one with '..' or '//' in it) disables the holder check entirely.", "scenario": "I ran with --root $D/linkroot, where linkroot is a symlink to $D/t. The tree t/held, which was the cwd of a running `sleep 600`, was deleted: the process's cwd then read '.../t/held (deleted)'. Every other tree under that root was deleted too.", "fix": "Apply os.path.realpath to each root before globbing, refuse a root that is itself a symlink or that resolves to a different path, and compare holders against the resolved candidate path.", "verified": "ran code: test tree with --apply"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 149, "severity": "major", "problem": "Holder detection misses relative-path users. A process whose cwd is above the candidate and refers to it as 'x/...' only in argv, or keeps no fd open between reads, matches nothing (cwd, exe, maps or the absolute form of argv) and is treated as not using the tree.", "scenario": "I ran `cd $R && python3 rel/serve.py`, a loop that rereads rel/data.txt every second. The script is closed after loading and exe is /usr/bin/python3. Both with --apply and on the second, symlink-free run, rel/ was deleted while the process was alive. The process continued, but its data file was gone. The same applies to any long-lived server or daemon started from /tmp with relative arguments.", "fix": "Resolve each relative argv item against the process's cwd (os.path.normpath(os.path.join(cwd, item))) before matching. Also treat a process whose cwd is an ancestor of the candidate (cwd == root) as suspicious when its argv names the candidate's basename.", "verified": "ran code: test tree with --apply and a real live process"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 99, "severity": "major", "problem": "Purpose miss: any socket file anywhere in a tree blocks deletion forever, even a dead one nobody listens on. Orchestra's own pytest runs leave dead sockets (notify-ready.sock), and so does Chromium (SingletonSocket). So the biggest worker scratch, the ~3.5 GB/day this script exists for, is never cleaned automatically.", "scenario": "Real dry run: /tmp/orchestra-v1170 (3116 MB) is kept with reason 'внутри сокет ... popen-gw0/test_ready_notification_is_sen0/notify-ready.sock'. 4629 org.chromium.Chromium.* dirs are kept for the same reason, and /tmp holds 4992 entries. After 11-03 18:54, orchestra-v1170 moves to the owner bucket. bg_jobs does not deduplicate pattern matches (app/bg_jobs.py, around line 1205), so every janitor run will send the same TMP_JANITOR alert until a human acts.", "fix": "Count a socket as used only if its path is bound in /proc/net/unix (column 8). Dead socket inodes are just files, which matches 'used by no process'. This reading of the owner's 'do not touch sockets' needs the owner's confirmation. Also deduplicate owner-bucket alerts, e.g. with a state file and re-alerting once a day.", "verified": "ran code: dry run on the real /tmp; read bg_jobs.py"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 121, "severity": "minor", "problem": "As kesha, the process scan silently skips the cwd, root, exe, fd, maps and environ of other users' processes. Only cmdline is world-readable. The docstring and KB claim that 'ни один процесс' references the tree, which is unverified for most processes.", "scenario": "Live VPS: 292 processes, 201 with unreadable cwd (173 root, 8 www-data, 3 each for uids 1002, 999 and 1001). A root or www-data process using kesha's /tmp/x through cwd, an fd, or a config file rather than argv is invisible, so /tmp/x gets deleted. One example would be nginx root /tmp/x/site, or a sudo-started tool with cwd /tmp/x.", "fix": "State the limitation in the KB and the docstring. Optionally count unreadable processes and print them, or match their readable cmdline against both the absolute path and the basename. Fully closing this needs CAP_SYS_PTRACE, or running the check as root, which is the owner's call.", "verified": "ran code: counted unreadable /proc entries on the VPS"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 103, "severity": "minor", "problem": "Mount handling: (a) a bind mount of the same filesystem has the same st_dev and is not detected, so rmtree descends into it and deletes the bind source; (b) for a different-device mount (e.g. rclone FUSE), the walk keeps descending into it on every run.", "scenario": "(a) Someone with sudo runs `mount --bind /home/kesha/orchestra/data /tmp/exp/data` (/ and /tmp are both /dev/sda1) and forgets it. After 3 quiet days the janitor deletes the live data/ through the bind mount. (b) A forgotten rclone mount at /tmp/x/gdrive: every run lists the whole remote, up to 2M entries, burning API quota and time towards the 600 s kill.", "fix": "Read /proc/self/mountinfo once and skip any candidate that contains a mount point (by path prefix). Do not descend into directories whose st_dev differs from the parent's.", "verified": "reasoning only, confirmed by reading the code. kesha cannot mount without root and there is no FUSE mount on the VPS now"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 96, "severity": "minor", "problem": "'Touched' ignores reads. A tree that is only read or executed periodically, by a cron script or a tool run from /tmp, has old mtime and ctime and no process between runs, so it is deleted although it is still in use. Relatime atime on regular files would show the use; the janitor's own lstat does not update file atime.", "scenario": "/tmp/tool/run.py is run once a day by a user crontab for 4+ days without writing anything. On the next janitor run between executions it is deleted, and the cron job starts failing.", "fix": "Include st_atime of regular files (not directories, whose atime the janitor's own scandir refreshes) in newest, or document that read-only use does not count.", "verified": "reasoning only"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 209, "severity": "minor", "problem": "Future timestamps: a tree whose ctime or mtime lies in the future, for example after the clock was set back, stays in kept with 'менялось меньше срока назад' forever. It never reaches the owner bucket. Conversely, a forward clock jump makes everything look old at once.", "scenario": "In my test, touch -d 2027-06-01 future/f gave a kept tree that is never reported. After an NTP fix that sets the clock back a year, every file written before the fix stays invisible to cleanup and reporting.", "fix": "Treat newest > now + 1 day as suspicious and put large trees in the owner bucket with a 'время в будущем' reason.", "verified": "ran code: test tree"}, {"file": "scripts/tmp_janitor.py", "line": 221, "severity": "minor", "problem": "There is a race between the re-check and rmtree, and refs_source() is rescanned once per deletion. A worker that reuses a deterministic scratch name (/tmp/orchestra-v1170 when V-1170 resumes) can write into the tree while rmtree runs, which takes seconds for GB trees. Its new files are deleted, or rmtree fails half-way. The per-deletion /proc rescan makes the run O(deletions x processes).", "scenario": "A worker runs `mkdir -p /tmp/orchestra-v1170/full && cp -a src ...` while the janitor is removing that 3 GB tree. The worker's copy disappears mid-test, or the error bucket shows OSError 'Directory not empty' with a half-deleted tree. Rescan cost: with ~400 deletable codex_review_* files at 0.07 s per scan this adds ~30 s, fine today. It approaches the 600 s limit only with ~1000+ busy processes.", "fix": "Before rmtree, atomically rename the candidate into a janitor-owned trash name, then re-check and delete. Workers then cannot hit the old name; if one recreates the name, it gets a new directory. Reuse a single refs snapshot plus a cheap re-check of PIDs that are new since that snapshot.", "verified": "reasoning only, confirmed by reading the code; the scan was timed on the VPS"}], "checked_ok": ["Symlink candidates at top level are skipped. Symlinks inside a tree are not followed: outside/precious and the symlinked outside dir survived --apply.", "Hardlinks: removing the /tmp link leaves the outside copy (outside/hl survived). Harmless; only the reported bytes are overstated.", "rmtree on the VPS Python 3.12.3 is fd-based: _use_fd_functions=True, avoids_symlink_attacks=True. Sticky /tmp stops other users from swapping kesha's entries.", "Holder detection works for absolute references: process cwd inside the tree (t2/held kept); a venv with a symlinked python started by a relative path from cwd inside the tree (kept through cwd); the real /tmp/v322venv servers (in cmdline, 43 maps entries each). Deleted-but-open files match because the match boundary is '/'.", "_mentions boundaries: /tmp/x does not match /tmp/x.log, /tmp/x-old or /var/tmp/x. Characters outside the safe set (=, :, space, non-ASCII) cause conservative matches, not misses.", "Dot entries (/tmp/.X11-unix etc.) are never candidates. systemd-private-*, tmux-*, *.lock and similar names are silently skipped. The photobooth systemd-private-* dirs that vanish mid-run end up as 'не читается' and are kept.", "FIFOs, sockets and device nodes block deletion (FIFO tree went to owner). Unreadable subdirs, foreign-owned files and different st_dev block deletion.", "The MAX_ENTRIES cut-off only sets skip, so a truncated tree is never deleted. Its newest and bytes in the owner report come from the partial walk only.", "Dry run against the real /tmp and /var/tmp: 42 items, 0.00 GB, all small old /var/tmp files (b222-*, task2-*, task303-*, stt_new.py, sttpatch, kesha-ghidra, ...) plus the empty /tmp/seedon-V429-embedded-qhz73dcm. None was in use or recent, so each is acceptable under the owner's rule. Large trees (V-480-merge-audit 2.2 GB, v1180-* up to 573 MB, v322venv) are currently kept only because they are less than 3 days old.", "Performance: 18.8 s cold, 2.6 s warm, max RSS 23 MB. The /proc scan of 125 readable processes and 12.7k refs takes 0.07 s, and holders() runs only for stale, non-skipped candidates.", "A partial rmtree failure goes to the errors bucket and exit code 1. The next run sees a fresh ctime on the parent and waits another 3 days. Nothing worse happens.", "tests/test_tmp_janitor.py: 19 passed on the VPS worktree."], "extra": "Commands used: dry run `python3 scripts/tmp_janitor.py` in /home/kesha/ops-worktrees/orchestra-disk-guard. Test trees were in /home/kesha/janrev.qsBSJY (mktemp -d), driven through janitor.run(apply=True, now=time.time()+4*86400) with the real _process_refs. The directory was removed afterwards, and the leftover test `sleep 600` (cwd inside it) was killed by exact PID after checking its cwd and cmdline. Root-level paths that the fixes for defects 1 and 4 would affect in the real /tmp: /tmp/orchestra-v1170 (3116 MB, dead pytest socket), /var/tmp/orchestra-v1170-matched2-20261031/baseline (nested repo), /tmp/kesha (nested repo kin/ plus personal PDF and JPG; I suggest the owner add .orchestra-keep there or move the files)."}

